1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,952
The message reaches Timur on a Friday afternoon.

2
00:00:03,002 --> 00:00:08,712
An engineer on the ecom side has made a private key for ecom's calls into the card zone.

3
00:00:08,762 --> 00:00:10,944
Shall I just email it over, he asks?

4
00:00:10,994 --> 00:00:13,896
Timur's reply is one word: never.

5
00:00:13,946 --> 00:00:19,536
In Lesson 12, a caller passed the mail slot only with a certificate from the card zone.

6
00:00:19,586 --> 00:00:24,480
This lesson shows how a caller gets that certificate, while its secret key stays at home.

7
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:26,592
Think about what that email would do.

8
00:00:26,642 --> 00:00:30,096
One product's secret would sit in another product's inbox.

9
00:00:30,146 --> 00:00:33,264
Whoever holds that key can pretend to be the caller.

10
00:00:33,314 --> 00:00:37,512
I think a private key that has travelled is a key you no longer own.

11
00:00:37,562 --> 00:00:40,392
The right way works like a passport application.

12
00:00:40,442 --> 00:00:44,736
You send a photo, the office stamps it, and it never needs your face.

13
00:00:44,786 --> 00:00:48,696
First, the caller makes its private key inside its own Vault.

14
00:00:48,746 --> 00:00:51,024
The key stays there, and never leaves.

15
00:00:51,074 --> 00:00:54,264
From that key, the caller makes an application form.

16
00:00:54,314 --> 00:00:57,696
The form carries the key's public half and the caller's name.

17
00:00:57,746 --> 00:01:00,960
That form is called a certificate signing request.

18
00:01:01,010 --> 00:01:03,120
It is the only thing the caller sends.

19
00:01:03,170 --> 00:01:09,648
On the other side sits the card zone's certificate authority: the one the card zone trusts to stamp certificates.

20
00:01:09,698 --> 00:01:11,520
It checks the form.

21
00:01:11,570 --> 00:01:13,392
Is it well made?

22
00:01:13,442 --> 00:01:15,264
Does it carry the right name?

23
00:01:15,314 --> 00:01:18,432
Is that name on the card zone's list of callers?

24
00:01:18,482 --> 00:01:21,072
If so, it signs it, for a limited time.

25
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:25,560
The signed certificate is good only for calling in, never for serving.

26
00:01:25,610 --> 00:01:29,280
Then one folder goes back to the caller, with four things inside.

27
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:31,152
The signed certificate.

28
00:01:31,202 --> 00:01:35,808
The authority's own seal, so the caller can recognise the real card zone.

29
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:40,272
A secret for the login server, which travels by its own secure channel.

30
00:01:40,322 --> 00:01:42,144
And the addresses to call.

31
00:01:42,194 --> 00:01:44,784
The caller stores everything in its own Vault.

32
00:01:44,834 --> 00:01:47,352
Then it deletes the loose secret file.

33
00:01:47,402 --> 00:01:53,880
From then on, every call it makes carries two proofs: the certificate, and a token from the login server.

34
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:59,184
And every certificate is watched, so a warning goes out well before it expires.

35
00:01:59,234 --> 00:02:03,672
There is no portal for this, so here is what the two operators did that Friday.

36
00:02:03,722 --> 00:02:08,184
On the ecom side, the installer made the key and wrote out one small form.

37
00:02:08,234 --> 00:02:12,648
The engineer carried that form, and only that form, to Timur.

38
00:02:12,698 --> 00:02:14,520
Timur signed it.

39
00:02:14,570 --> 00:02:21,072
Had the name not been a listed caller, the signing would have stopped with a clear refusal, and nothing would have been issued.

40
00:02:21,122 --> 00:02:26,808
The name was listed, so a folder appeared, named for ecom, with the four things inside.

41
00:02:26,858 --> 00:02:29,040
The engineer carried the folder back.

42
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:32,712
The secret went separately, by a secure channel.

43
00:02:32,762 --> 00:02:36,096
On the ecom side, the installer read the folder.

44
00:02:36,146 --> 00:02:40,608
It wrote it all into ecom's Vault, and removed the secret file from disk.

45
00:02:40,658 --> 00:02:44,064
The first call went through the mail slot, and the card zone answered.

46
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:50,064
Months later, Timur's team sees a warning that the certificate is close to its end date.

47
00:02:50,114 --> 00:02:54,168
They repeat the same steps, and the old certificate retires on time.

48
00:02:54,218 --> 00:02:56,712
Nobody is woken by a sudden outage.

49
00:02:56,762 --> 00:03:00,048
Each product keeps its own key, in its own Vault.

50
00:03:00,098 --> 00:03:03,312
A leak on one side never exposes the other.

51
00:03:03,362 --> 00:03:06,456
The card zone decides who may call, and for how long.

52
00:03:06,506 --> 00:03:09,600
The caller never touches the card zone's keys.

53
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:15,168
And for the bank's audit, every certificate has one owner, one signer, and one end date.

54
00:03:15,218 --> 00:03:19,536
Next: how the card zone stops a thief testing stolen cards.
